WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.747
E23 and E24 had you write a tool as a user, saved into your own settings.

2
00:00:02.747 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode is the other path — adding a tool that ships with the app itself, the way a real pull request would.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.925
Two genuinely different things share the word tool.

4
00:00:08.925 --> 00:00:10.962
E23's JavaScript tool lives entirely in your settings.

5
00:00:10.962 --> 00:00:13.302
json — the model never sees your source, and it's yours alone.

6
00:00:13.302 --> 00:00:19.000
A built-in tool group lives in the repo, ships to every install, and is what E22's Files, Terminal, Web and Browser groups actually are under the hood.

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:19.223
tools.

8
00:00:19.223 --> 00:00:20.040
ts is where both live.

9
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:25.463
BUILTIN_TOOLSETS at the top groups tool ids into the named sets you saw in the tools panel — Browser, Research, Coding agent, Self-service, Swarm.

10
00:00:25.463 --> 00:00:32.000
Further down, each individual tool has a name, a description — the exact text the model reads to decide when to use it, same rule as E23 — and a JSON Schema for its parameters.

11
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:32.592
toolDiscovery.

12
00:00:32.592 --> 00:00:38.592
ts is the other half — it's what powers the self-service tooling group from E25, letting the model turn a tool on for itself mid-conversation.

13
00:00:38.592 --> 00:00:44.000
A RISKY set flags anything destructive, like run_terminal, so those always prompt for approval instead of silently switching on.

14
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.584
Add a minimal one live: word_count, taking a single text string parameter and returning a count.

15
00:00:47.584 --> 00:00:51.168
Write the description the way E23 taught — specific about when to use it, not just what it does.

16
00:00:51.168 --> 00:00:58.000
Then find where tool names are dispatched to their actual implementation and add the matching case; a definition with no implementation is a tool the model can call that does nothing.

17
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Wire it into a group by adding its id to a toolIds array in BUILTIN_TOOLSETS — a tool with a definition but no group membership never appears in the tools panel at all, because the panel lists groups, not individual tool ids.

18
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.936
Reload the app, open a chat's tools panel, and there it is — inside its group, same as Files or Web, indistinguishable from a tool that's been there since day one.

19
00:01:15.936 --> 00:01:19.000
That's the whole test: does it show up exactly where a built-in belongs.

20
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Next, the same contribution exercise on the UI layer — extending an existing small panel with a real feature, wired properly into the store from E66.

